---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import HeroBanner from '../components/HeroBanner.astro';

const title = 'Portugal Highly Qualified Activity Visa - Official Name and Meaning';
const metaTitle = `${title} | Portugal HQA Residency`;
const metaDescription =
  'Portugal Highly Qualified Activity visa is the official 2026 name for HQA residence. This page defines the term and points you to the full program guide.';
---

<Layout title={metaTitle} description={metaDescription}>
  <article>
    <header>
      <HeroBanner
        image="/images/portugal-highly-qualified-activity-visa-overview.jpg"
        alt="Aerial view of Porto’s Douro riverfront at sunset, with cruise boats on the water and terracotta-roofed houses along Ribeira"
        title={title}
        items={[
          { href: '/', label: 'Home' },
          { href: '/portugal-hqa-visa', label: 'Portugal HQA Visa' },
          { label: 'Highly Qualified Activity visa' },
        ]}
      />
      <div class="bg-background">
        <div class="site-container py-24 md:py-32">
        <p class="max-w-3xl">
          Portugal Highly Qualified Activity visa is the official name for the residence permit that English-language practice shortens to HQA. The abbreviation is a label, not a separate product. If a contract, a consular checklist, or a host letter says “highly qualified activity,” it is pointing at this permit type, not at a Golden Visa subscription and not at a passive-income D7 file.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-background-alt">
      <div class="site-container max-w-3xl py-24 md:py-32">
        <section>
          <h2>What the official name refers to</h2>
          <p class="mt-6">
            In Portuguese law and in AIMA and consular English, the route is described as residence for highly qualified activity. “Visa” in search queries usually means the entry visa plus the residence permit that follows, not a tourist sticker. The person must have a qualifying professional activity and a Portuguese host. The name does not create a capital-investment path and does not replace the tests on the
            <a href="/portugal-hqa-visa-requirements" class="inline-link">HQA visa requirements</a>
            page.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            You will also see “highly qualified activity residence permit,” “HQA residence,” and, in legal files, an Article 90 citation. Those strings sit on the same route. The
            <a href="/portugal-article-90-residence-permit" class="inline-link">Portugal Article 90 residence permit</a>
            page explains the statutory heading. This page stops at the name.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-24">
          <h2>How HQA is used in everyday filings</h2>
          <p class="mt-6">
            Employers, universities, and research units often write HQA in emails because it is short. The supporting pack still has to match the full name: evidence that the activity is highly qualified, that the host is a Portuguese entity that can support the file, and that the applicant meets identity, record, and subsistence rules in force at filing. A letter that only says “HQA candidate” is not a legal category on its own.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4">
            Researchers who arrived on a “research visa” search should not treat that phrase as a second scheme. Many hosted research posts are assessed as highly qualified activity. The
            <a href="/portugal-research-visa" class="inline-link">Portugal research visa</a>
            page covers that search intent without repeating the program guide.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-24">
          <h2>Where the full program is documented</h2>
          <p class="mt-6">
            Eligibility audiences, cost structure, family coverage, and how a file is assembled belong on the canonical
            <a href="/portugal-hqa-visa" class="inline-link">Portugal HQA visa</a>
            guide. Use that page when you need the route, not the dictionary entry. Confirm current AIMA and consular wording before you treat any English nickname as the form title on a 2026 filing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <a href="/portugal-hqa-visa" class="card cluster-card mt-16 flex flex-col p-8 transition-[transform,box-shadow,border-color] duration-300 ease-out hover:-translate-y-[5px] hover:border-brand hover:shadow-[0_12px_32px_rgba(28,25,23,0.10)]">
          <h3>Portugal HQA visa program guide</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 flex-1">
            Role, host, costs, family coverage, and the application path for highly qualified activity residence.
          </p>
          <span class="btn-primary mt-8 w-full gap-2">
            Open the HQA visa guide
            <span class="cluster-card-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="bg-cta-tint">
      <div class="site-container flex flex-col items-start gap-6 py-24 md:py-32">
        <h2 class="max-w-3xl">Check whether the official name matches the role and the host letter</h2>
        <p class="max-w-2xl">
          This page is informational. It is not legal, tax, or immigration advice. An advisor can compare the English nickname on your documents with the permit heading AIMA and the consulate will use.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact" class="btn-cta">Discuss whether this is the right permit name</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
